arly results of 203 patients: retrospective study</article-title><source>Hong Kong Med J</source><year>2002</year><volume>8</volume><fpage>322</fpage><lpage>328</lpage><!--PubMed citation query: 'Hong Kong Med J||8|322||bib54|'--><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12376708</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib55"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Zahl</surname><given-names>PH</given-names></name><name><surname>Strand</surname><given-names>BH</given-names></name><name><surname>Maehlen</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Incidence of breast cancer in Norway and Sweden during introduction of nationwide screening: prospective cohort study</article-title><source>BMJ</source><year>2004</year><volume>328</volume><fpage>921</fpage><lpage>924</lpage><!--PubMed citation query: 'BMJ||328|921||bib55|'--></citation></ref></ref-list></back><floats-wrap><fig id="fig1"><label>Figure 1</label><caption><p>Trends in incidence rates of invasive (<bold>A</bold>) and <italic>in situ</italic> breast cancer (<bold>B</bold>) according to histologic subtype, based on the Swedish Family-Cancer Database. Incidence rates are adjusted for age (European standard). The mean annual increase or decrease is presented at the curve. The numbers of observed cases are presented for each year.</p></caption><graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" mime-subtype="gif" xlink:href="92-6602250f1"/></fig><fig id="fig2"><label>Figure 2</label><caption><p>Relative risk of contralateral (<bold>A</bold>) and ipsilateral (<bold>B</bold>) invasive breast carcinoma subsequent to <italic>in situ</italic> breast carcinoma for different periods.</p></caption><graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" mime-subtype="gif" xlink:href="92-6602250f2"/></fig><table-wrap id="tbl1" position="float"><label>Table 1</label><caption><p content-type="table-title">Contralateral invasive breast cancer subsequent to <italic>in situ</i